THOUGHT: Now let me understand the vulnerability in `parse_content_length`. The issue is the multi-line header format. Let me think about the parsing logic:

```c
p = buffer;
/* search the begining of the number */
while ( p<end && (*p==' ' || *p=='\t' ||
(*p=='\n' && (*(p+1)==' '||*(p+1)=='\t')) ))
        p++;
```

This skips leading spaces/tabs, and also `\n` followed by space/tab (multi-line continuation).

```c
/* parse the number */
size = 0;
number = 0;
while (p<end && *p>='0' && *p<='9') {
        number = number*10 + (*p)-'0';
        size ++;
        p++;
}
```

This parses digits.

```c
/* now we should have only spaces at the end */
while ( p<end && (*p==' ' || *p=='\t' ||
(*p=='\n' && (*(p+1)==' '||*(p+1)=='\t')) ))
        p++;
if (p==end)
        goto error;
/* the header ends proper? */
if ( (*(p++)!='\n') && (*(p-1)!='\r' || *(p++)!='\n' ) )
        goto error;
```

The vulnerability: The multi-line format. Consider a Content-Length header like:
```
Content-Length: 5\r\n 10\r\n
```

